From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: emuTOS-MCD <darekadumon@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 9 Aug 2026 06:29:51 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 011/190] segacd: publish the BRAM filesystem signature, off
 the timer register
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hardware showed the B: driver was never called — the last block request
was a clean read of A: — so GEMDOS is refusing the drive before any
sector I/O, which is what a failed BPB parse looks like (and explains
"0 bytes"). The driver now reads the boot sector back out of the BRAM
after formatting and publishes it, so the screen can show whether the
filesystem is really there: 0x6010 is correct.

The first attempt put that word at 0xFF8030, which is past the eight
sub->main comm registers and lands on the gate array's timer; all eight
words were already spoken for, so it shares 0xFF8022 with the old
engine status instead.
---
 bios/segacd.c | 10 ++++++++++
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+)

diff --git a/bios/segacd.c b/bios/segacd.c
index dd826db..4c9c672 100644
--- a/bios/segacd.c
+++ b/bios/segacd.c
@@ -275,6 +275,14 @@ static void bram_probe(void)
     bram_state = BRAM_OURS;
 }
 
+/* Read back the bytes GEMDOS's getbpb depends on, so the status line can
+ * show whether the filesystem we wrote is actually there. Expect 0x6010:
+ * 0x60 = the boot sector's first byte, 0x10 = 16 total sectors. */
+static UWORD bram_fs_signature(void)
+{
+    return (UWORD)((bram_rd(0) << 8) | bram_rd(19));
+}
+
 void segacd_floppy_init(void)
 {
     /* the builder stamps "EmuTOSMD" as the OEM field at offset 2 */
@@ -307,6 +315,8 @@ void segacd_floppy_init(void)
     /* low nibble: state; bit 4: writes verified */
     *(volatile UWORD *)0xFF802A =
         (UWORD)(bram_state | (bram_wtest ? 0x10 : 0));
+    *(volatile UWORD *)0xFF8022 =
+        (bram_state == BRAM_NONE) ? 0 : bram_fs_signature();
 }
 
 BOOL segacd_flop_detect_drive(WORD dev)
